@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Indexes Introduction";
}

<div class="container-fluid">
    <div class="row">

        <!-- =========================
             MAIN CONTENT
        ========================== -->

        <div class="col-lg-12">

            <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 mb-4">

                <div class="card-body p-4">

                    <!-- =========================
                         TITLE
                    ========================== -->

                    <h1 class="fw-bold mb-4">
                        Indexes Introduction
                    </h1>


                    <!-- =========================
                         INTRODUCTION
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
                        What is an Index?
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        An <strong>Index</strong> in SQL Server is a database object
                        used to improve the speed of data retrieval operations.
                        Indexes help SQL Server find records faster without scanning
                        the entire table.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        An index works similar to an index in a book. Instead of
                        checking every page to find a particular topic, we can use
                        the index to quickly locate the required information.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         WHY INDEXES ARE USED
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Why are Indexes Used?
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Indexes are mainly used to improve the performance of
                        SELECT queries and make data retrieval faster.
                    </p>

                    <ul>
                        <li>Indexes improve query performance.</li>
                        <li>They help SQL Server find records quickly.</li>
                        <li>They reduce the amount of data SQL Server needs to scan.</li>
                        <li>Indexes are useful for columns frequently used in WHERE clauses.</li>
                        <li>They can improve JOIN and ORDER BY operations.</li>
                        <li>Indexes can also improve searching and sorting operations.</li>
                    </ul>


                    <!-- =========================
                         SIMPLE EXAMPLE
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Simple Example
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Suppose we have a <strong>Students</strong> table:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        Suppose the table contains thousands of student records
                        and we frequently search students based on the
                        <strong>City</strong> column.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        Creating an index on the <strong>City</strong> column can
                        help SQL Server find the required records more efficiently.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         CREATING INDEX
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Creating an Index
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        A non-clustered index can be created using the
                        <strong>CREATE INDEX</strong> statement.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students(City);</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        Here:
                    </p>

                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <strong>IX_Students_City</strong> is the name of the index.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Students</strong> is the table name.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>City</strong> is the column on which the index is created.
                        </li>
                    </ul>


                    <!-- =========================
                         TYPES OF INDEXES
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Types of Indexes
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        SQL Server supports different types of indexes.
                        The commonly used types are:
                    </p>

                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Clustered Index</strong>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Non-Clustered Index</strong>
                        </li>
                    </ul>

                    <p>
                        In the upcoming topics, we will learn about both
                        clustered and non-clustered indexes in detail.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         CLUSTERED INDEX
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Clustered Index
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        A <strong>Clustered Index</strong> determines the physical
                        order of data in a table.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A table can have only one clustered index because the
                        physical data can be stored in only one order.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE CLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_StudentId
ON Students(StudentId);</code></pre>


                    <!-- =========================
                         NON-CLUSTERED INDEX
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Non-Clustered Index
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        A <strong>Non-Clustered Index</strong> is a separate
                        structure that stores indexed column values along with
                        references to the actual rows in the table.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A table can have multiple non-clustered indexes.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Students_City
ON Students(City);</code></pre>


                    <!-- =========================
                         INDEX AND SELECT QUERY
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        Index and SELECT Query
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Consider the following query:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT StudentId,
       StudentName,
       City,
       Age
FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

                    <p class="mt-3">
                        If an appropriate index exists on the
                        <strong>City</strong> column, SQL Server may use the
                        index to locate matching records more efficiently.
                    </p>


                    <!-- =========================
                         IMPORTANT POINTS
                    ========================== -->

                    <div class="alert alert-warning mt-5">

                        <h4 class="fw-bold">
                            Important Points
                        </h4>

                        <ul class="mb-0">
                            <li>
                                Indexes are mainly used to improve data retrieval performance.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Indexes are useful for frequently searched columns.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                A table can have only one clustered index.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                A table can have multiple non-clustered indexes.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Indexes require additional storage space.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Too many indexes can affect INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE performance.
                            </li>
                        </ul>

                    </div>


                    <!-- =========================
                         WHEN TO USE INDEXES
                    ========================== -->

                    <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5 mb-3">
                        When Should We Use Indexes?
                    </h2>

                    <p>
                        Indexes should be considered when a table contains a
                        large amount of data and specific columns are frequently
                        used for searching, filtering, joining or sorting.
                    </p>

                    <ul>
                        <li>Columns used frequently in WHERE conditions.</li>
                        <li>Columns frequently used in JOIN conditions.</li>
                        <li>Columns frequently used for sorting.</li>
                        <li>Columns frequently used for searching.</li>
                    </ul>


                    <!-- =========================
                         INDEX PERFORMANCE NOTE
                    ========================== -->

                    <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">

                        <h5 class="fw-bold">
                            Remember
                        </h5>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            Indexes can significantly improve SELECT query
                            performance, but they also require storage and can
                            increase the cost of INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE
                            operations. Therefore, indexes should be created
                            carefully.
                        </p>

                    </div>


                    <!-- =========================
                         SUMMARY
                    ========================== -->

                    <div class="alert alert-primary mt-5">

                        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                            Summary
                        </h2>

                        <p>
                            Indexes are database objects used to improve the
                            performance of data retrieval operations in SQL Server.
                            They help SQL Server find records faster instead of
                            scanning the entire table.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            The two commonly used types of indexes are
                            <strong>Clustered Index</strong> and
                            <strong>Non-Clustered Index</strong>.
                            A table can have one clustered index and can have
                            multiple non-clustered indexes.
                        </p>

                        <p class="mb-0">
                            In the next topic, we will learn about
                            <strong>Clustered Index</strong> in detail.
                        </p>

                    </div>


